0..73 Comment "/// A lambda over a composite path, against the composite of two lambdas."
74..77 Comment "///"
78..163 Comment "/// `q ↦ (q then g) then h` and the composite of `q ↦ q then g` and `r ↦ r then"
164..243 Comment "/// h` are one function. Each body agrees with the other once the machine takes"
244..287 Comment "/// the bind at the endpoint of `q then g`."
288..291 Comment "///"
292..374 Comment "/// The composite\'s body binds `q then g`, a transport stuck on `g`, into `t ↦ t"
375..452 Comment "/// then h`. That transport returns the value it moves, whatever its path. So"
453..532 Comment "/// the bind is its continuation at that endpoint, the transport along `h` that"
533..613 Comment "/// the lambda writes. Inside an action\'s motive, the kernel reads the two codes"
614..668 Comment "/// by its own rules, which run the composite\'s calls."
669..672 Comment "///"
673..745 Comment "/// - see: #3824 — a call of a returning variable held by the same law"
746..824 Comment "/// - see: #4011 — the checker refused the composite against its two lambdas"
825..905 Comment "/// - see: #4005 — the action over the composite, which the code walk hands on"
907..945 Comment "/// The composite of `F` and then `G`."
